1
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:05,640
Two retirees with the same balance, the
same withdrawals and

2
00:00:05,660 --> 00:00:07,140
the same average return.

3
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:11,020
One ran out of money in 1991.

4
00:00:12,270 --> 00:00:14,230
So what decided it?

5
00:00:17,930 --> 00:00:20,630
It is January 1966.

6
00:00:21,030 --> 00:00:26,510
The first retiree starts with $1 million,
measured in 1966 dollars.

7
00:00:26,910 --> 00:00:32,630
They retire at the start of a hard decade
for markets, though nobody knows it yet.

8
00:00:33,030 --> 00:00:39,458
By the end of this video, you will know
which ten years decided the fate of this

9
00:00:39,478 --> 00:00:45,290
retiree, and whether the same thing held
for every start year since 1928.

10
00:00:45,690 --> 00:00:51,430
The money sits in a portfolio of 60%
stocks and 40% bonds.

11
00:00:51,830 --> 00:00:57,250
The stocks are the Standard & Poor's 500
index, with dividends reinvested.

12
00:00:57,650 --> 00:01:00,750
The bonds are 10-year US Treasury bonds.

13
00:01:01,150 --> 00:01:05,250
Every January, the portfolio is rebalanced
back to that mix.

14
00:01:05,650 --> 00:01:10,970
In the first year, the retiree withdraws
4% of the starting balance.

15
00:01:11,370 --> 00:01:16,130
That is $40,000, in 1966 dollars.

16
00:01:16,530 --> 00:01:22,431
After that, the withdrawal rises each year
with the previous year's inflation, so it

17
00:01:22,451 --> 00:01:24,610
always buys the same groceries.

18
00:01:25,010 --> 00:01:27,490
In real terms, it never changes.

19
00:01:27,890 --> 00:01:32,990
In nominal terms, the dollars of the day,
it climbs every year.

20
00:01:33,390 --> 00:01:38,630
The plan runs for 30 years, from 1966
through 1995.

21
00:01:39,030 --> 00:01:40,970
No taxes, no fees.

22
00:01:41,370 --> 00:01:47,130
We keep the model this bare on purpose, so
that one thing can change at a time.

23
00:01:47,530 --> 00:01:53,330
The data is US only, and this is history,
not a forecast.

24
00:01:54,330 --> 00:01:56,290
Now meet the second retiree.

25
00:01:56,690 --> 00:02:00,756
The mirror retiree lives through the exact
same 30 annual

26
00:02:00,776 --> 00:02:02,890
returns, but in reverse order.

27
00:02:03,290 --> 00:02:10,190
The return of 1995 arrives first, and the
return of 1966 arrives last.

28
00:02:10,590 --> 00:02:15,710
This retiree is illustrative: no one lived
through these years in this order.

29
00:02:16,110 --> 00:02:21,179
Inflation stays in its real calendar
order, so both retirees take out the

30
00:02:21,199 --> 00:02:22,850
same dollars every year.

31
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:26,250
Here is the question this first part
answers.

32
00:02:26,650 --> 00:02:32,330
If two people earn the same average
return, do they end up in the same place?

33
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:38,890
The portfolio of the 1966 retiree earned
an average return of 9.7%.

34
00:02:40,390 --> 00:02:45,230
For the mirror retiree, the average is
also 9.7%.

35
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:47,810
Not close. Identical.

36
00:02:48,210 --> 00:02:52,981
That is the geometric average, the
compound rate that turns the starting

37
00:02:53,001 --> 00:02:54,970
dollar into the ending dollar.

38
00:02:55,370 --> 00:03:00,033
Multiplication does not care about order,
so the same years in any

39
00:03:00,053 --> 00:03:02,010
order give the same average.

40
00:03:02,410 --> 00:03:09,410
Even the simple arithmetic average, adding
up the 30 returns and dividing by 30, is

41
00:03:09,562 --> 00:03:11,690
the same for both: 10.3%.

42
00:03:12,090 --> 00:03:13,150
Same money in.

43
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:14,750
Same money out.

44
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:16,390
Same average.

45
00:03:16,790 --> 00:03:19,930
The only thing that differs is the order.

46
00:03:24,330 --> 00:03:26,850
Watch the two balances year by year.

47
00:03:27,250 --> 00:03:31,698
The question now: when does the order
start to matter,

48
00:03:31,718 --> 00:03:33,830
and how much does it cost?

49
00:03:34,230 --> 00:03:39,210
Year one. The 1966 retiree loses 4.8%.

50
00:03:39,610 --> 00:03:45,910
The mirror retiree gets the 1995 return
first: a gain of 31.7%.

51
00:03:46,310 --> 00:03:49,370
One year in, and the paths have already
split.

52
00:03:49,770 --> 00:03:51,830
Then inflation starts to climb.

53
00:03:52,230 --> 00:03:58,570
By 1969 it is 6.2% a year, and every
withdrawal grows with it.

54
00:03:58,970 --> 00:04:04,930
After inflation, stocks and bonds both
lose money in 1973 and 1974.

55
00:04:05,330 --> 00:04:12,330
In 1974 alone, the portfolio loses 14.7%,
while prices rise 12.3%.

56
00:04:13,690 --> 00:04:20,690
In 1966 dollars, the 1966 retiree ends
1974 with $461,000.

57
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:28,487
The mirror retiree, same year, same
withdrawals: $1.27

58
00:04:28,507 --> 00:04:31,140
million, also in 1966 dollars.

59
00:04:31,540 --> 00:04:35,820
Here is why a bad start is so hard to
undo.

60
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:42,293
The withdrawal is still $40,000 in 1966
dollars, but it is now taken

61
00:04:42,313 --> 00:04:44,520
from a much smaller pile.

62
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:49,241
Every dollar withdrawn after a loss is a
dollar that is not there

63
00:04:49,261 --> 00:04:50,840
when the recovery comes.

64
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:53,620
Analysts call this sequence-of-returns
risk.

65
00:04:54,020 --> 00:04:55,680
Good years do come.

66
00:04:56,080 --> 00:05:03,080
In 1975 the portfolio gains 23.6%, and in
1976 another 20.7%.

67
00:05:04,560 --> 00:05:08,620
But they compound on a balance that has
already been cut, while the

68
00:05:08,640 --> 00:05:10,080
withdrawals keep rising.

69
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:13,940
Through the rest of the decade, inflation
keeps rising.

70
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:18,200
In 1979 it reaches 13.3%.

71
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:25,600
By 1981, the yearly withdrawal has grown
to $108,553 in nominal dollars.

72
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:32,880
In 1966 dollars, it is still $40,000.

73
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:38,180
Then, in 1982, the good years finally
arrive.

74
00:05:38,580 --> 00:05:43,220
That year the 1966 retiree gains 25.4%.

75
00:05:43,620 --> 00:05:48,340
But the withdrawal now takes 16.9% of what
is left.

76
00:05:48,740 --> 00:05:55,487
In 8 of its years with gains above 10%,
the balance of the 1966 retiree still fell

77
00:05:55,507 --> 00:05:58,360
after the withdrawal and inflation.

78
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:00,740
The boom comes too late.

79
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:06,820
For the mirror retiree, those same years
came first, when the balance was largest.

80
00:06:07,220 --> 00:06:09,420
The gap widens every year.

81
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:11,940
In 1986 it peaks.

82
00:06:12,340 --> 00:06:19,340
In 1966 dollars, the mirror retiree is now
ahead by $1.96 million.

83
00:06:21,140 --> 00:06:26,589
At $40,000 a year in 1966 dollars, that
gap would pay for 49

84
00:06:26,609 --> 00:06:28,940
more years of withdrawals.

85
00:06:30,740 --> 00:06:35,360
From there, the story of the 1966 retiree
is short.

86
00:06:35,760 --> 00:06:42,492
The mirror retiree meets the bad years
late: the 1974 loss arrives in 1987, on a

87
00:06:42,512 --> 00:06:45,180
balance big enough to absorb it.

88
00:06:45,580 --> 00:06:52,580
The account pays out its last $96,829, in
nominal dollars, and hits zero in 1991.

89
00:06:56,950 --> 00:07:01,897
That last payment is short of the full
withdrawal, and the final 4 years

90
00:07:01,917 --> 00:07:03,550
of the plan get nothing.

91
00:07:03,950 --> 00:07:10,950
The mirror retiree finishes 1995 with
$6.96 million in nominal dollars.

92
00:07:11,550 --> 00:07:18,550
In 1966 dollars that is $1.44 million,
more than the starting balance, after

93
00:07:18,945 --> 00:07:21,250
30 years of withdrawals.

94
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:25,310
Both portfolios averaged 9.7% a year.

95
00:07:25,710 --> 00:07:28,890
The average did not decide who went broke.

96
00:07:33,290 --> 00:07:35,530
So was 1966 a fluke?

97
00:07:35,930 --> 00:07:42,070
We ran the same rules for every start year
from 1928 to 1996.

98
00:07:42,470 --> 00:07:46,770
That is 69 retirements, each with 30 years
of data.

99
00:07:47,170 --> 00:07:51,030
The question for this part: what actually
decides the outcome?

100
00:07:51,430 --> 00:07:54,250
Most of these retirees never ran out.

101
00:07:54,650 --> 00:08:00,230
In 65 of the 69 start years, the money
lasted all 30 years.

102
00:08:00,630 --> 00:08:07,630
A retiree who started in 1982 under the
same rules ended with $5.36

103
00:08:07,888 --> 00:08:10,450
million in 1982 dollars.

104
00:08:10,850 --> 00:08:16,870
Even a retiree who started in 1929, the
year of the crash, never ran out.

105
00:08:17,270 --> 00:08:23,080
Prices fell through the Depression, so the
withdrawals shrank, and after inflation

106
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:26,190
the first decade still averaged 3.9% a
year.

107
00:08:26,590 --> 00:08:31,430
For many start years, the order of returns
did no harm at all.

108
00:08:31,830 --> 00:08:34,770
Surviving is not the same as thriving.

109
00:08:35,170 --> 00:08:41,075
In 26 of the 69 start years, the retiree
ended with less than the starting

110
00:08:41,095 --> 00:08:43,070
balance, after inflation.

111
00:08:43,470 --> 00:08:50,090
4 start years ran out of money, beginning
with 1965 and 1966.

112
00:08:50,490 --> 00:08:53,310
Then 1968 and 1969.

113
00:08:53,710 --> 00:08:56,750
Their long-run averages do not single them
out.

114
00:08:57,150 --> 00:09:04,150
After inflation, the 1966 retiree averaged
4.1% a year over all 30 years.

115
00:09:05,630 --> 00:09:11,610
17 start years had a lower real average
than that, and never ran out.

116
00:09:12,010 --> 00:09:17,430
After inflation, the 1969 retiree averaged
5.6% a year.

117
00:09:17,830 --> 00:09:22,870
The 1928 retiree averaged less: 4.9%.

118
00:09:23,270 --> 00:09:30,270
Yet 1928 ended with $1.21 million in 1928
dollars, and 1969 ran out

119
00:09:30,967 --> 00:09:33,910
of money in its 28th year.

120
00:09:34,710 --> 00:09:38,130
What the four failures share is their
first decade.

121
00:09:38,530 --> 00:09:43,231
In every one of them, the portfolio lost
money after inflation

122
00:09:43,251 --> 00:09:45,050
over its first 10 years.

123
00:09:45,450 --> 00:09:52,450
After inflation, the first decade of the
1966 retiree averaged −1.8%.

124
00:09:54,600 --> 00:10:00,240
In the first decade, the mirror retiree
averaged 6.9% a year after inflation.

125
00:10:00,640 --> 00:10:02,620
So, what decided it?

126
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:04,200
Not the average.

127
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:06,240
The first 10 years.

128
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:12,474
To be precise: across all 69 start years,
the 30-year real return still lines up

129
00:10:12,494 --> 00:10:15,240
best with how much is left at the end.

130
00:10:15,640 --> 00:10:20,140
But every retiree who ran out met a bad
first decade.

131
00:10:20,540 --> 00:10:26,417
The 9.7% average described the 1966
retiree perfectly, and it

132
00:10:26,437 --> 00:10:28,720
said nothing about 1991.

133
00:10:30,370 --> 00:10:32,770
Now the limits of this analysis.

134
00:10:33,170 --> 00:10:35,390
This is history, not a forecast.

135
00:10:35,790 --> 00:10:40,150
It is US only, one country with one set of
markets.

136
00:10:40,550 --> 00:10:45,793
The 69 windows overlap, so 98 years of
data hold only 3

137
00:10:45,813 --> 00:10:48,350
separate 30-year stretches.

138
00:10:48,750 --> 00:10:55,750
And the model is bare: no taxes, no fees,
one portfolio mix and one withdrawal rule.

139
00:10:56,450 --> 00:10:59,950
Change any of those, and every number here
changes too.

140
00:11:01,150 --> 00:11:07,196
The data: annual returns compiled at NYU
Stern, and consumer prices from the Bureau

141
00:11:07,216 --> 00:11:09,310
of Labor Statistics via FRED.

142
00:11:09,710 --> 00:11:13,410
Returns include dividends and bond
coupons, and the portfolio

143
00:11:13,430 --> 00:11:14,990
is rebalanced once a year.

144
00:11:15,390 --> 00:11:18,970
Every number and formula is listed in the
description.

145
00:11:22,770 --> 00:11:28,610
Two retirees, one average, and a fate
decided by the order of the years.
